<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Danish Kingdom of East Anglia</h1><p class="st-meta">879 CE – 918 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Viking kingdom founded by Guthrum in East Anglia after his defeat by Alfred, the first Danish state in England to accept Christianity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Alfred defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-edington">Edington</a> in 878, the Danish leader accepted baptism with the name Aethelstan. In 879 his army settled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-east-anglia">East Anglia</a>, the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-east-anglia">Kingdom of East Anglia</a>, and a treaty with Alfred around 886 fixed the boundary along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-lea">Lea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-great-ouse">Ouse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watling-street">Watling Street</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guthrum">Guthrum</a> ruled as a Christian king and minted coins under his baptismal name. Danish settlers took land across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, and soon after his death coins honouring the martyred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-the-martyr">King Edmund</a> were issued, showing rapid integration. Later rulers, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eohric">Eohric</a>, are poorly documented.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The kingdom joined attacks on English territory, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> conquered it in 917 to 918, and the East Anglian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> submitted. It became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-anglo-saxons">Kingdom of the Anglo-Saxons</a> and later an earldom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 879 CE – 918 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.19%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>879 CE – 918 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guthrum, Eohric</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse, Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guthrum's Kingdom, Viking East Anglia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=898&amp;focus=danish-kingdom-of-east-anglia" aria-label="Danish Kingdom of East Anglia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4653 309 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Danish Kingdom of East Anglia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5022 623h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4973 565h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4973 565h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=898&amp;focus=danish-kingdom-of-east-anglia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
